    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95952.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "For over a week now, Donald Trump's unconventional cabinet picks have been dominating coffee pot chatter here in Washington. You've got Pete Hexeth, the Fox News darling, who was a decorated army soldier, but isn't exactly known for his high-ranking military credentials, yet Trump wants him to run the Pentagon. Then there's Robert F. Kennedy, the vaccine skeptic who Trump wants to lead the nation's top health department. And don't forget Democrat termed Magga Sweetheart, Tulsi Gabbard, who Democrats actually refer to as a Russian agent, but who Trump wants to lead national intelligence. The conventional wisdom in Washington has been that Republicans will fall online, no matter how much they actually hate these picks. Trump, after all, is the reason they hold their majorities in Congress. His victory was decisive, in all or at least most of his critics, have been pushed out of office with their tails between their legs. But yesterday we got a glimpse that this take actually might be wrong. Matt Gates, his firebrand pick for Attorney General, actually withdrew his name from consideration. You may think it was due to all the headlines about his sexual relations with a minor, or doing drugs, or attending sex parties. Nope, it was actually Senate Republicans back-channelling to Trump about their concerns. It just so happens that when this news broke, I was sitting across from one of them. Senator Kevin Kramer, a North Dakota Republican who just days ago was on a plane lobbying Trump to consider a Plan B for Attorney General. Turns out, he wasn't alone. Lots of Republicans were doing this behind the scenes. Using their relationship with Trump, cultivated over the past eight years to implore him to reconsider. Senator Kramer and I caught up in his office Thursday, where we talked about Trump's most controversial nominees and how they may fare in the confirmation process. I saw his real-time reaction to the Gates News. and got a detailed download on specifically what Kramer thinks HEGS-F needs to do to actually have a shot at getting confirmed. And I heard his take on how Senate Republicans' relationships with Donald Trump have improved and how that could make the party more effective this time around. Kramer also held up the Gates Inclusion as an example of how Senate Republicans still effectively have their backbone. Sure, the party will be deferential to Trump, but that doesn't mean they're going to fall in mind with everything Trump wants.",
