  {
    "episode_id": 95952,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95952.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 137,
    "turn_text": "I think that the botched withdrawal from Afghanistan was one of the greatest embarrassments this country's ever been through, but I don't lay that on officers. I lay that on the Commander-in-Chief. This was his decision, his decision only, his leadership at the time, and I would point specifically to Central Command Commander, General Frank McKenzie, Um, advised against it. We've had many generals with Mark Millie, Chairman of Joint Chiefs of Staff, advised against it. They both of them advised to maintain a troop count of about 2,500. If not, at least 1,800. He hopped it for none. And the timing couldn't have been worse. This was Joe Biden's mess. I think we need to be very careful when we start going after military officers and personnel who saluted the Commander-in-Chief and saluted the Secretary of Defense. That's their job. They should not be blamed for that. No, to his credit, Frank McKenzie is such an upstanding former commander that he takes the blame. Ironically, he has said the buck stops with me, particularly as it relates to the 13 Marines that were killed. In reality, it wasn't his fault at all. He didn't make the call. But that's what heroes do. Cowards hide behind their predecessor in the White House and say it was his fault.",
