  {
    "episode_id": 95952,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95952.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 159,
    "turn_text": "Oh, I have not. I've not talked to him about it, but I've been very open about it. Again, it gets back to that fundamental constitutional responsibility of advising consent. I think for a cabinet secretary, it's hard for me to imagine a scenario. where a Republican Congress would allow a recess appointment when we actually have the majority to make the decision. I can understand why a president might make a recess appointment if he didn't have the majority. In other words, if a cabinet secretary nominee didn't have the support of a majority of the members of the United States Senate, you'd never be a very strong cabinet secretary if he had a recess appointment. So, I think when you have the same party, it doesn't make a lot of sense. Now, 1,200 confirmations is a lot of confirmations. Plus, all of the advancements, we vote on every general officer promotion in the military. We vote on every advancement or promotion in the State Department. I mean, there's thousands of them in a certain year or term. It's very conceivable that you could have a recess appointment of a number of people at a sub cabinet level. I do think a cabinet level is different. And I don't want to say, again, I try to avoid saying what I'll never do, but it should be very rare if ever.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Advising consent is a fundamental constitutional responsibility.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "I have not spoken to him about it.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Cabinet-level appointments differ significantly from sub-cabinet-level appointments in terms of appropriateness for recess appointments.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "A president might make a recess appointment if they lack a Senate majority.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Recess appointments at the sub-cabinet level are plausible given the volume of confirmations required.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "I have been very open about it.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "A cabinet secretary appointed via recess appointment would likely lack strength due to lack of Senate support.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "It is difficult to imagine a Republican Congress allowing a recess appointment when the president's party has a Senate majority.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Recess appointments at the cabinet level should be extremely rare, if not nonexistent.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The legitimacy of a cabinet secretary depends on formal Senate confirmation.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Senate majority status reflects political alignment between the executive and legislative branches.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
